# The brand short links and the docs move, as a Cloudflare assets redirect list.
#
# These were `next.config.ts` `redirects()`, and before that a `_redirects` on the
# Cloudflare Pages site that used to serve the apex. They are back in the file
# Cloudflare reads directly: every one of them is a published URL — in the README,
# in the install instructions, on the socials, in the docs' own cross-links — so
# they are part of the deployed contract and not a convenience. A plain file is
# also the one form where "what does /x do in production" is answerable by reading
# rather than by building.
#
# 302 throughout, never 301. A 301 is cached by the browser more or less forever,
# every one of the social links points at a third-party account that can move, and
# the docs have just moved origin once already. A social handle is not a canonical
# URL change.
#
# `/install` is deliberately absent: it is a real script at `public/install`,
# served as a static asset. A redirect would break `curl -fsS` without `-L`.

/github         https://github.com/sigiroai              302
/x              https://x.com/SigiroAI                   302
/twitter        https://x.com/SigiroAI                   302
/linkedin       https://www.linkedin.com/company/sigiro  302
/discord        https://discord.gg/sigiro                302

# Blume generates these from the same content, so they live beside it. The paths
# stay reachable here because the footer, the README and the llms.txt convention
# itself all point at the site root.
#
# Above the splats below on purpose: Cloudflare matches this list in order and warns
# at build time that a static rule sitting under a splat rule is slower to serve.
/llms.txt       https://docs.sigiro.com/llms.txt         302
/llms-full.txt  https://docs.sigiro.com/llms-full.txt    302

# The docs are Blume at docs.sigiro.com, where they sit at the root rather than
# under /docs. `/docs/api/*` has no per-operation equivalent — the reference is one
# generated page from the live spec — so every operation lands on it. That rule must
# come before the general `/docs/*`, because these are matched in order.
/docs                   https://docs.sigiro.com          302
/docs/api/*             https://docs.sigiro.com/api      302
/docs/*                 https://docs.sigiro.com/:splat   302
